------------------------------------------------------------------------

This appendix reports the full model output supporting the meso-level analysis of cultural immigration attitudes. All models use individual and wave fixed effects with standard errors clustered at the respondent level. The outcome throughout is `immigCultural`, which asks respondents whether immigration undermines or enriches British cultural life; higher values indicate more permissive attitudes. The structure mirrors Appendix B exactly: Section B.8.1 presents the main Britishness models for all three constituency-level economic indicators; Section B.8.2 reports the categorical Britishness robustness check; Section B.8.3 presents the parallel Englishness models; and Section B.8.4 reports the categorical Englishness robustness check. A description of the meso-level variables appears in Section B.8.5.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B.8.1 Main Models: Britishness (Continuous) {#sec-b8-brit}

## B.8.1.1 Local Unemployment Rate {#sec-b8-brit-unemp}

Across the baseline specifications, the local unemployment rate exhibits no statistically significant main effect on cultural immigration attitudes once individual and wave fixed effects are included. Once the interaction with Britishness is introduced, however, a conditional pattern emerges that differs in structure from the immigSelf results in Appendix B.1: the unemployment main effect is positive and significant (β ≈ 0.039, p \< 0.001), indicating that higher local unemployment is associated with more permissive cultural attitudes at the highest levels of Britishness, while the negative and significant interaction term (β ≈ −0.006, p \< 0.001) indicates that this association attenuates as Britishness decreases. The Britishness main effect is positive and significant in the interaction models. This pattern is robust to the inclusion of age controls.

```{r b8-brit-unemp-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Unemp only"    = m_u0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness" = m_u1,
    "(3) Unemp + Age"   = m_u0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness" = m_u2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", unemployment_rate = "Unemp. rate (local)", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.1. Local Unemployment Rate and Cultural Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigCultural.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b8-brit-unemp-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_u3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_u4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", unemployment_rate = "Unemp. rate (local)",
                  "brit:unemployment_rate" = "Britishness × Unemp. rate", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.2. Local Unemployment Rate × Britishness: Interaction Models (Cultural DV)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigCultural.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

## B.8.1.2 Claimant Count {#sec-b8-brit-claimant}

Results for claimant count reveal a conditional structure that is also directionally distinct from the immigSelf claimant models. Main-effect specifications show no significant unconditional association between claimant intensity and cultural immigration attitudes. In the interaction models, the claimant main effect is negative and marginally significant (β ≈ −0.022, p \< 0.05), the Britishness main effect is negative and significant (β ≈ −0.018, p \< 0.001), and the interaction term is positive and significant (β ≈ 0.004, p \< 0.01), indicating that the negative claimant association attenuates as Britishness increases. This pattern is consistent across age-controlled and uncontrolled specifications.

```{r b8-brit-claimant-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Claimant only"  = m_c0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness"  = m_c1,
    "(3) Claimant + Age" = m_c0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness"  = m_c2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", claimant_rate = "Claimant count (local)", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.3. Claimant Count and Cultural Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigCultural.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b8-brit-claimant-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_c3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_c4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", claimant_rate = "Claimant count (local)",
                  "brit:claimant_rate" = "Britishness × Claimant count", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.4. Claimant Count × Britishness: Interaction Models (Cultural DV)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigCultural.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

## B.8.1.3 Local Income (ASHE) {#sec-b8-brit-income}

Local income shows no significant main effect or meaningful interaction with Britishness in the cultural DV specifications. Neither the baseline nor the interaction models provide evidence that changes in local-authority earnings predict within-person changes in cultural immigration attitudes, and the interaction term is non-significant across both specifications.

```{r b8-brit-income-main}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Income only"   = m_i0.1,
    "(2) + Britishness" = m_i1,
    "(3) Income + Age"  = m_i0.2,
    "(4) + Britishness" = m_i2
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", earnings_median = "Local income (ASHE)", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.5. Local Income (ASHE) and Cultural Immigration Attitudes: Main Effects (Britishness)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigCultural.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

```{r b8-brit-income-int}
modelsummary(
  list(
    "(1) Interaction"       = m_i3,
    "(2) Interaction + Age" = m_i4
  ),
  coef_rename = c(brit = "Britishness", earnings_median = "Local income (ASHE)",
                  "brit:earnings_median" = "Britishness × Local income", age = "Age"),
  gof_map = GOF_MAP,
  stars   = c("*" = .05, "**" = .01, "***" = .001),
  title   = "Table B8.6. Local Income (ASHE) × Britishness: Interaction Models (Cultural DV)",
  notes   = "Individual and wave FE. SEs clustered by respondent. DV: immigCultural.",
  escape  = FALSE,
  output  = "kableExtra"
) |> kable_styling()
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B.8.2 Robustness: Britishness (Categorical) {#sec-b8-brit-cat}

Re-estimating the cultural DV meso models using Britishness as a categorical variable (reference: 7) broadly confirms the conclusions from the continuous specifications, though the gradient structure differs from the immigSelf categorical results in Appendix B.2. Britishness category coefficients in the main-effects models are positive for some categories but not uniformly significant or monotonically ordered, reflecting the weaker unconditional Britishness effect on cultural attitudes relative to immigSelf.

For local unemployment, categorical interaction models reveal significant positive interaction terms concentrated at intermediate and stronger identity positions (brit=3 through brit=6), indicating that the positive within-person unemployment association with permissive cultural attitudes is present across most identity positions but is largest at the reference category. For claimant count, significant negative interactions are concentrated at the weakest identity positions (brit=1 and brit=2), consistent with the continuous interaction result and indicating that the negative claimant association is most pronounced — and least attenuated by identity — among the weakest Britishness respondents. Local income yields no coherent pattern of categorical interactions, consistent with the null in the continuous specifications.

